<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Fast DDS profile TEMPLATE for the WSL2 path: Isaac Sim native on WINDOWS, Hydra in a WSL2
  Ubuntu 24.04 distro on the SAME PC. The Orin is not in this path at all (Hydra with
  seg_source:=gt + pose_source:=isaac_gt reads only Isaac's own topics).

  Why this exists: Hydra needs Ubuntu 24.04 + ROS Jazzy + GTSAM/PCL, so it cannot run on Windows,
  where Isaac must run natively (Docker Desktop/WSL2 does not expose the RTX renderer - see
  output/hil/run_windows.md). WSL2 is therefore the only place for Hydra on this machine, and the
  two halves have to meet over DDS across the vEthernet (WSL) NAT.

  ONE template renders BOTH sides; only the substitutions differ (the placeholders are written
  without their at signs here, so the renderer does not substitute them into this comment):
    Isaac (Windows): OWN_IP = the vEthernet (WSL) address, PEER_IP = the Ubuntu address
    Hydra (WSL):     OWN_IP = the Ubuntu address,          PEER_IP = the vEthernet (WSL) address
  Rendered by scripts/sim/win/hil_win.py (net=wsl) and scripts/hydra/wsl_dds_profile.sh.
  Never write the addresses in here by hand. (A literal double hyphen is illegal in an XML comment
  body and makes Fast DDS discard the whole profile without a word: CLAUDE.md, "Packaging".)

  BOTH addresses change every time the WSL VM restarts, so this is rendered AT EVERY LAUNCH. Read
  them from inside WSL, where one call gives both (the default gateway IS the Windows side):
    own  = hostname -I | awk '{print $1}'
    peer = ip route show default | awk '{print $3}'

  Why not the profiles that already exist:
  - isaac_local.xml whitelists 127.0.0.1 only. WSL2 is a separate network namespace behind NAT, so
    loopback never reaches it.
  - hil.xml.in points every participant at the Discovery Server on the Orin, which is not running
    for this path.

  Why explicit unicast initial peers instead of multicast: WSL2 sits behind the NAT of the
  vEthernet (WSL) adapter and multicast across it cannot be relied on. Unicast initial peers need
  no multicast at all, and two hosts are too few to be worth a Discovery Server process.

  The participant lease is the stack's own 5 s / 1 s, as in every other profile here. The lease is
  announced by each participant itself, so BOTH sides must set it (CLAUDE.md, "DDS / discovery").

  Socket buffers: the OS maximum must be at least @BUFFER_BYTES@ on BOTH sides, or Fast DDS
  silently gets a small buffer and drops the ~15 datagrams of a 0.92 MB image in bursts. A WSL
  distro starts at net.core.rmem_max = 212992 (208 kB), 80x too small; with systemd=true a file in
  /etc/sysctl.d survives a restart, unlike the `sysctl -w` line setup.md gives for the Orin.

  Windows firewall: the inbound direction (WSL -> Windows) is filtered by Windows, and the
  existing HIL rules are per address pair (192.168.200.2 with the Thor and the Orin), so they do
  not cover the 172.x WSL network. The WSL side has no firewall at all (no ufw, no iptables
  binary). See output/hil/hydra_wsl_status.md.
-->
<profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <transport_descriptors>
    <transport_descriptor>
      <transport_id>f1t_udp_wsl</transport_id>
      <type>UDPv4</type>
      <maxMessageSize>65000</maxMessageSize>
      <sendBufferSize>@BUFFER_BYTES@</sendBufferSize>
      <receiveBufferSize>@BUFFER_BYTES@</receiveBufferSize>
      <!-- The Windows side has many adapters (Ethernet 2 and 3, Tailscale, VMnet1, VMnet8,
           vEthernet (WSL)). Without this list Fast DDS announces locators on all of them and the
           peer may pick one it cannot route to. 127.0.0.1 stays so that tools on the same host
           (hil_topics.bat, hil_hz.bat) still see the graph. -->
      <interfaceWhiteList>
        <address>@OWN_IP@</address>
        <address>127.0.0.1</address>
      </interfaceWhiteList>
    </transport_descriptor>
  </transport_descriptors>
  <participant profile_name="f1tenth_wsl" is_default_profile="true">
    <rtps>
      <builtin>
        <discovery_config>
          <leaseDuration><sec>5</sec></leaseDuration>
          <leaseAnnouncement><sec>1</sec></leaseAnnouncement>
        </discovery_config>
        <!-- No port given: Fast DDS probes the well-known ports of participants
             0..maxInitialPeersRange (default 4) at each address. @OWN_IP@ is listed too, so two
             participants on the same side still find each other without multicast. -->
        <initialPeersList>
          <locator><udpv4><address>@PEER_IP@</address></udpv4></locator>
          <locator><udpv4><address>@OWN_IP@</address></udpv4></locator>
        </initialPeersList>
      </builtin>
      <userTransports>
        <transport_id>f1t_udp_wsl</transport_id>
      </userTransports>
      <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
    </rtps>
  </participant>
</profiles>
